insert into public.presentation_references (slot, rank, image_url, page_url, site, why, shot) values
  ('what_vesper_is.hero', 1, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/hi-res-242.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/gallery/', 'Gemstone Lights (brand gallery, Architectural tab)', 'The page captions it ''Family home using warm white architectural lights''. It is a 2000x1333 landscape frame of an ordinary family home, not an estate, lit in the everyday warm white mode rather than a holiday pattern. Gemstone was founded in Calgary in 2016 and puts its lights in a track under the soffit, so the product looks closest to Vesper''s: a row of separate points rather than a strip. I found the file through the gallery''s lightbox link and caption, and the link is live. I did not view the pixels: I downloaded no images, and WebFetch could not reach the host, so I read the page HTML through the shell proxy. Check the sky colour, framing and which runs are lit by eye before copying it.', 'Tripod on the far sidewalk across the street at chest height, levelled, 1x lens, straight on, 15 to 30 minutes after that day''s sunset while the sky is deep blue. Warm white steady on every run: eaves, front gable and the garage line. Frame the house across the middle 70% of the width, keep the whole roofline inside the middle 60% of the height, leave a strip of sky above the peak and the empty driveway at the bottom. Porch light off, at most one or two lamps on inside, garage closed, no house number or plates in frame. Hold AE/AF lock on the eave lights and pull exposure down (about -1 to -2 EV) until every node is its own point, not a line. Landscape, at least 2732 px wide.'),
  ('what_vesper_is.hero', 2, 'https://astorialightingco.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/Sectors-Residential-scaled.jpg', 'https://astorialightingco.com/', 'Astoria Lighting Co. (installer, homepage Residential tile)', 'Backup for the timing. The alt text reads ''A two-story house at dusk with Astoria Lighting illuminating its exterior'', so it matches the two-storey form and the dusk sky. Astoria also sells landscape lighting, so the caption does not confirm that only the roofline is lit. It is likely a US (Texas) home. I confirmed it on the page by file and alt text; I did not view the pixels.', 'Same as the main shot: across the street, chest height, 1x, straight on, 15 to 30 minutes after sunset. Copy only the sky timing. Keep every non-roofline light (porch, landscape) off so the eaves, gable and garage runs carry the frame.'),
  ('hidden_by_day.night', 1, 'https://www.oelo.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/horsetooth-header-nite-3.jpg', 'https://www.oelo.com/', 'Oelo (brand homepage hero)', 'The homepage hero has two slides of the same house, titled ''Invisible by day.'' (horsetooth-header-day-1.jpg) and ''Expressive by night.'' (horsetooth-header-nite-3.jpg). Only the light changes between them, which is the logic of Vesper''s day/night slider. I confirmed both files and the slide text in the page source. I did not view the pixels, and the night slide may be in colour rather than warm white.', 'Leave the tripod, framing, zoom and focus exactly as they were for the day frame. Shoot 15 to 30 minutes after sunset with warm white steady on every run. Press and hold on the eave lights for AE/AF Lock and pull exposure down until each node is a point. Porch light off. Same resolution and orientation as the day frame so the two line up to the pixel in the slider.'),
  ('hidden_by_day.night', 2, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/architectural-after-1.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/permanent-outdoor-lights/holiday-lighting/architectural-lighting/', 'Gemstone Lights (Architectural Lighting page)', 'This is the same interaction Vesper uses: a horizontal drag slider (offset 0.5) pairing architectural-before.jpg with architectural-after-1.jpg, labelled Before and After. The page copy says you''ll ''only notice your lights at night''. I confirmed the slider markup and both files in the page source and both links are live. I did not view the pixels, so I can''t confirm whether the Before frame is daylight or the house at dusk with the lights off.', 'Pair this frame with the day frame from the identical, untouched tripod spot. Warm white steady, 15 to 30 minutes after sunset, exposure locked on the lights, porch light off. Do not re-level or re-zoom between the two frames.'),
  ('whites_first.warm', 1, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/DSC_1263.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/gallery/', 'Gemstone Lights (brand gallery, Warm White tab)', 'The caption reads ''A close-up of a roofline, featuring warm white lights'' (2000x1331). It is a close roofline shot rather than the whole house, which is the scale where warm and cool white can be compared: the points, the glow on the soffit and the wall colour all show. A second close-up in the same tab, DSC_6672.jpg (''A close-up of the roofline of a home illuminating warm white lights''), is an alternate. For the pairing idea, see Gemstone''s ''Warm White or Cool White LEDs'' post (gemstonelights.com/warm-white-or-cool-white-leds/), which gives warm as 2000 to 3000K and cool as 3000 to 4500K. I confirmed the file and caption on the page; I did not view the pixels.', 'Tripod straight on to one front corner and a window at about 6 m, 1x, 15 to 30 minutes after sunset, warm white steady. Before shooting, set white balance to daylight (not auto), then press and hold on the lights for AE/AF Lock. Shoot the warm frame. Then, without touching the phone or tripod, switch the system to cool white and shoot the matching frame so the pair differs only in colour temperature.'),
  ('phone_control.screens', 1, 'https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/PurpleSource211/v4/3e/95/98/3e95985c-6932-0c35-351f-397251673a99/Simulator_Screenshot_-_iPhone_16_Pro_Max_-_2025-04-21_at_12.46.03.png/460x998bb-60.jpg', 'https://apps.apple.com/us/app/id1612868160', 'JellyFish Lighting (App Store listing, JellyFish Designer Cloud)', 'The iPhone screenshots on this listing are named ''Simulator_Screenshot_-_iPhone_16_Pro_Max'', so they are raw full-screen captures rather than designed marketing panels. That is the format the slot asks for: straight screens with no device frame. The listing has seven of them, showing the pattern lists and controls. By contrast, Gemstone''s listing uses files named ''AppStore_6_5_0X'', which look like designed panels. I confirmed the files in the listing source; I did not view the pixels.', 'Take two or three screenshots on Keenan''s own phone with side + volume up: (1) the looks list, (2) the lights on, (3) the lights off or the on/off control. Turn on Do Not Disturb first, clear notifications, and keep anything personal or from other apps off screen. Use the PNGs at native resolution with no device frame or mockup added.'),
  ('gallery.bi_level', 1, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/DSCF0728.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/gallery/', 'Gemstone Lights (brand gallery, Warm White tab)', 'I found no photo labelled bi-level or split-entry on any brand or installer page I could open. Trimlight''s gallery loads its photos by script, Jellyfish''s gallery has no captions, and installer pages turned up nothing labelled bi-level. This is the closest by caption: ''Warm white lights illuminating the entrance of a home at night'' (2000x1333). It is warm white and framed around the entrance, the feature that tells the viewer the house is a bi-level, from a brand founded in Calgary. Copy the treatment (warm white, entry emphasis), not the house. I confirmed it on the page; I did not view the pixels.', 'Blue hour, warm white steady, from the public sidewalk about 15 to 30 degrees off straight-on toward the entry side so the split entry (door, landing and steps between the two levels) reads clearly, 1x. Whole front lit: upper eaves, gable and any lower or garage run. Porch light off, no house number or plates, exposure held down so each node stays a point.'),
  ('gallery.bi_level', 2, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/bungalow-with-natural-white-lights-1024x800.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/warm-white-or-cool-white-leds/', 'Gemstone Lights (blog: Warm White or Cool White LEDs)', 'Backup for house shape only. The alt text reads ''bungalow with natural white lights'', and from the street a bi-level reads like a raised bungalow, so the proportions are close. The light is natural white, not warm, so do not copy the colour. I confirmed it on the page; I did not view the pixels.', 'Use it only for proportion and how much ground and sky to leave around a wide, low house. Shoot in warm white steady at blue hour, as in the main bi-level shot.'),
  ('gallery.holiday', 1, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/DSC_1183.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/gallery/', 'Gemstone Lights (brand gallery, Christmas tab)', 'The caption reads ''red and green Christmas lights display against a setting sky'' (2000x1331, landscape). The holiday colours are shot while the sky still has colour (blue hour) rather than against black, so the house and roofline stay readable and the colours don''t bloom. I confirmed it on the page; I did not view the pixels.', 'Christmas look (red, green and warm white in blocks), set to steady with no chase or twinkle. Blue hour, tripod across the street, 1x, whole front in frame. Hold AE/AF lock on the brightest colour (usually the white or green block) and pull exposure down until each colour is a clean, separate point. Porch light off, no house number. Place it last in the gallery order.'),
  ('gallery.holiday', 2, 'https://trimlight.com/hs-fs/hubfs/game-day-lighting.jpg?width=1341&height=894&name=game-day-lighting.jpg', 'https://www.trimlight.com/', 'Trimlight (brand homepage, Game Day tile; also on trimlight.com/locations/calgary)', 'Reference for the Game night variant. The alt text reads ''a home with white and orange LED''s with a blue sky and a moon'': two team colours in blocks, shot against a blue-hour sky. I confirmed it on both pages; I did not view the pixels.', 'If Game night is used instead of Christmas: red and amber in blocks, steady, blue hour, from across the street, whole front, exposed on the amber so the red does not clip. Same tripod spot as the other gallery frames. Last in the order.'),
  ('warranty.hero', 1, 'https://framerusercontent.com/images/EqdQTzg0En9AcwfDHRucipN0.jpeg?width=300&height=225', 'https://www.jellyfishlighting.com/blog/lighting-ideas-for-your-home-in-the-winter', 'JellyFish Lighting (blog: Lighting Ideas for Your Home in the Winter)', 'This is the only permanent-lighting photo with snow that I could confirm by caption: the alt text is ''House lights with snow'', under the heading ''Lighting Patterns for Snowy Weather''. It is small (300x225) and the copy says the pattern is shades of blue, not warm white. Use it for the mood of snow on the roof edge above the lights, not for colour or resolution. I confirmed it on the page; I did not view the pixels.', 'A clear evening after fresh snow, warm white steady, 15 to 30 minutes after sunset. Set white balance to daylight so the snow reads blue and the lights read warm. Stand in the driveway 3 to 4 m back, 1x or 2x, with an outside corner of the eave crossing the frame on a slight diagonal: the snow line along the roof edge above, a clean, even row of points under the soffit below. Keep the corner inside the middle 50% of the width for the tall crop. Porch light off, no house number.'),
  ('warranty.hero', 2, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/DSC_6672.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/gallery/', 'Gemstone Lights (brand gallery, Warm White tab)', 'Composition reference for the close eave-corner frame. The caption reads ''A close-up of the roofline of a home illuminating warm white lights'' (2000x1331). It is a close roofline shot in warm white from a brand that grew up in Calgary winters, the same scale and colour this slot needs. I found no snow photo of this type. I confirmed it on the page; I did not view the pixels.', 'Match this close roofline scale, then add the winter elements: fresh snow on the roof edge, blue sky about 20 minutes after sunset, eave corner on a slight diagonal kept in the middle 50% of the width, warm white steady, daylight white balance, AE/AF lock on the lights.'),
  ('what_vesper_is.more', 1, NULL, 'https://trimlight.com/blog/the-impact-of-trimlights-patented-bulb-design-on-your-home', 'Trimlight', 'Trimlight''s post on its bulb design is the brand''s close-up story. The lit points in the under-eave channel are the subject, which is what this slot needs. A close, low, raking view along the run makes each node read as its own point, while the soffit takes a soft wash and the channel stays a clean line. I only have the search description. trimlight.com is blocked in this session (DNS fails at the session''s network allowlist), and after jellyfishlighting.com failed the same way I stopped opening pages. So I have not seen this photo, and Keenan should check it shows lit nodes up close before copying it.', 'At dusk, warm white, steady, full brightness. Stand on the driveway about 2 m out, just below one front eave corner. iPhone at 1x (2x if the gutter corner pushes in), tilted up about 45 degrees and aimed along the run so the track leads away diagonally from the near corner. Frame only soffit, fascia edge and track with 6 to 8 nodes showing, and no downspout. Tap and hold on the nearest node to lock focus and exposure, then drag exposure down until the nodes are crisp separate dots, not blobs, and the soffit wash stays soft.'),
  ('what_vesper_is.more', 2, NULL, 'https://www.reviewed.com/smarthome/content/govee-permanent-outdoor-lights-2-pro-review', 'Reviewed.com review of Govee Permanent Outdoor Lights 2 Pro (photo credited to TJ Donegan)', 'Search results describe a reviewer''s own photo of the lights installed under a roof eave on a real house, taken from below. That is the angle this slot needs, and it is a real home, not a render. Govee uses clip-on pucks, not a track, so copy the angle and framing, not the hardware. Not opened (fetching stopped after the brand sites were blocked).', 'Same as the main shot: 2 m out below a front eave corner, 1x, about 45 degrees up along the run. Show soffit, fascia and Vesper''s track with 6 to 8 lit warm white nodes, exposed on the nearest node.'),
  ('hidden_by_day.more', 1, NULL, 'https://trimlight.com/support/how-does-trimlight-look-during-the-day', 'Trimlight', 'This is Trimlight''s answer to the objection this slot answers: what the track looks like by day. Its pages describe a colour-matched channel under the eave, behind the gutter and trim, that reads as a thin line from the ground. A viewer-height, open-shade view keeps the track small and honest against the soffit and fascia, and that is the point. Search description only; trimlight.com is blocked in this session, so I have not seen the photo.', 'Daylight in open shade (that side of the house out of direct sun, or an overcast day), lights off. Stand on the front walk 6 to 8 m out, phone at eye height, 1x, looking up at the eave over the front door or garage. Frame soffit and fascia across the width with the track as a thin line under the soffit in the middle third of the height. Keep it level and horizontal. No ladder, tools, cables, power supply or house number in frame.'),
  ('hidden_by_day.more', 2, NULL, 'https://trimlight.com/day-night', 'Trimlight', 'Trimlight''s day and night page is described as pairing a night shot and a day shot of the same home, with copy saying the colour-matched channel is close to invisible in daylight. The pair shows how a fixed position makes the lights the only difference. Use it to see how far back they stand and how much fascia they include. Not opened (blocked host).', 'From the same front-walk spot, 6 to 8 m out at eye height, 1x, shoot the eave by day with the lights off in open shade. Optionally come back at dusk to the same marked spot for a matching lit frame.'),
  ('whites_first.cool', 1, NULL, 'https://www.jellyfishlighting.com/warm-white', 'JellyFish Lighting', 'JellyFish''s white presets page is built around choosing a white from 2700K to 5000K presets on the same lights. A same-house, same-position comparison lets the eye judge only the colour of the light. Search description only; jellyfishlighting.com is blocked in this session, so I could not check whether it shows one house at several whites. Keenan should look for the same-house pair there.', 'Keep the tripod and framing from the warm white shot, with focus and exposure locked (AE/AF lock). In the app, switch to cool white at the same brightness and shoot within 30 seconds. Also lock white balance (a manual camera app, or RAW). Otherwise the iPhone''s auto white balance will pull cool white back towards neutral and hide the difference. Change nothing else.'),
  ('gallery.two_storey', 1, NULL, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/gallery/', 'Gemstone Lights (brand founded in Calgary)', 'Gemstone''s official gallery has a Warm White filter, and the brand started in Calgary. Its warm white homes are the best place to find the two-storey, front-garage suburban house Keenan knocks on. The look to copy is straight on, level and shot at blue hour: the whole roofline and garage line read as one continuous warm line against a deep blue sky, not black. Search description only; I did not open the gallery, so Keenan picks the specific photo there.', 'Blue hour, 15 to 30 minutes after sunset, warm white steady. Tripod across the street, directly opposite the middle of the house, at chest height, level (grid on), 1x. House across the middle 70% of the width, roofline inside the middle 60% of the height, whole house and garage line lit. Porch light off, driveway empty, house number out of frame. Expose for the lights so the sky stays blue.'),
  ('gallery.snow', 1, NULL, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/location/usa/california/lake-tahoe/', 'Gemstone Lights (Lake Tahoe location page)', 'Search results describe this page''s gallery as rooflines lit against snowy backgrounds. Snow on the roof and lawn throws the light back and shows the lights belong in winter, which is the proof a Calgary homeowner wants. At blue hour the snow reads blue and the warm white reads warm. Search description only; not opened.', 'The evening after fresh snow, before it is walked on or plowed, 15 to 30 minutes after sunset, warm white steady. Tripod across the street, 1x, level. Snow on the roof edge above the track and an untouched strip of lawn snow in the lower third. Check first that no snow or icicles hide the nodes. Expose for the lights, then bring exposure down until the snow is blue-grey, not blown white.'),
  ('gallery.snow', 2, NULL, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/winter-lighting-ideas/', 'Gemstone Lights (Winter Lighting Ideas guide)', 'Gemstone''s winter guide talks about white light reflecting off snow and warm white for a cozy look, so it is likely to carry the brand''s own winter house photos. Search description only; not opened.', 'Same as the main snow shot: across the street at blue hour after fresh snow, warm white steady, lights clear under the soffit.'),
  ('install_day.hero', 1, NULL, 'https://trimlightcalgary.com/about', 'Trimlight Calgary (local installer)', 'Search results describe this local installer''s About page as showing a crew working at a house''s eave and gutter line, and the company truck parked in front of a house. That is the real install-day story in Calgary, with the vehicle showing the crew is a proper business. Search description only; not opened.', 'Bright overcast daylight. A crew member in a Vesper jacket on a ladder fitting track under a front eave, a second person footing the ladder, both faces turned to the work (faces only with their OK). From the lawn at about 45 degrees to the wall, 2x. Ladder feet on mats, tidy driveway, Vesper work vehicle at the curb in frame. Keep the ladder and eave inside the middle 50% of the width so a tall crop holds them. Take a vertical frame as well.'),
  ('install_day.hero', 2, NULL, 'https://www.outdoorlights.com/greater-albany/residential/permanent-roofline-lighting/', 'Outdoor Lighting Perspectives of Greater Albany (installer)', 'Search results describe an image on this installer page labelled as an installation team working on a home''s roofline: crew at the eave, shot from the ground. Search description only; not opened.', 'Same as the main install shot: lawn at about 45 degrees, 2x, ladder and eave centred in the middle 50% of the width, footer on the ladder, vehicle at the curb.'),
  ('hidden_by_day.day', 1, 'https://trimlight.com/hubfs/day-shot-home-before.jpg', 'https://trimlight.com/day-night', 'Trimlight (brand page "Beautiful by Day, Brilliant by Night")', 'The brand''s own day and night pair of one house. day-shot-home-before.jpg is the lights-off daytime frame and Night-shot-home-after.jpg (https://trimlight.com/hubfs/Night-shot-home-after.jpg) is the lit frame, under copy saying the channel is "virtually invisible in daylight". That is this slot''s job: the day frame proves the track disappears when the lights are off, and the pair only works because both frames come from one locked camera spot, which is what the scenario asks for (mark the tripod spot and wait for dusk). Not viewed: I opened the page source and confirmed both files and the copy, and both image links return 200. I could not display the pixels in this session, so check the height and framing on screen before copying them.', 'Shoot on a bright overcast day, or with the front in open shade and the sun behind you. Lights off, garage door closed, driveway empty, bins and hoses put away. Set the tripod on the far sidewalk at chest height, level, at 1x, square to the house. Keep the whole roofline inside the middle 60% of the frame height, with some sky above and lawn below. Take the frame, tape the tripod feet on the sidewalk and leave zoom and framing alone. Come back 15 to 30 minutes after sunset for the matching warm white frame, so the meeting can show the same house by day and by dusk.'),
  ('hidden_by_day.day', 2, 'https://www.oelo.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/horsetooth-header-day-1.jpg', 'https://www.oelo.com/', 'Oelo (brand home page header)', 'Backup for the same slot. Oelo''s home page header is a slider titled HomeHeaderHorsetooth with two layers of one house: "horsetooth-header-day" (this file) and "horsetooth-header-nite" (https://www.oelo.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/09/horsetooth-header-nite-3.jpg). It fades the day frame into the night frame, which is a good model if the day frame on our page will cross-fade into Keenan''s dusk frame. Not viewed: confirmed from the page source and the link works. I could not see the image, so I don''t know its camera height or angle.', 'Same as the Trimlight entry above. Lock the tripod and keep zoom and framing identical between the day frame and the dusk frame, so the two line up exactly and nothing moves when one fades into the other.'),
  ('whites_first.hero', 1, 'https://astorialightingco.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/soffit-sample_3-scaled.jpg', 'https://astorialightingco.com/solutions/soffit-lighting/', 'Astoria Lighting Co. (installer, own closed-track soffit system; Product Showcase)', 'The caption on the installer''s showcase matches this slot almost word for word: "White vinyl siding home featuring Astoria''s soffit-fitted permanent lighting system, illuminating the entryway and garage with warm white light that enhances architectural details". It is warm white only, the entry and garage are together in frame, and the light washes down siding from a track under the soffit, like Vesper''s. Not viewed: I confirmed the caption on the page and the link returns 200, but not the camera angle. Avoid Astoria''s blog images named "ArtificialSEO": they look AI-made.', 'Shoot 15 to 30 minutes after sunset. Every node on steady warm white (white channel only, no RGB mixed in), porch light off, front door closed. Stand at the end of the driveway at a three-quarter angle, so the garage eave runs away from you toward the front entry. Use 2x and let the garage eave and entry fill the frame. Tap the lit siding under the eave and lower the exposure until the wash on the siding or stone keeps its texture and the light points don''t blow out. Brace the phone or use a small tripod, and turn Night mode off if it smears the points.'),
  ('whites_first.hero', 2, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/20May27-184-600x600.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/permanent-outdoor-lights/accent-lighting/', 'Gemstone Lights (brand architectural lighting gallery)', 'Backup. The caption in Gemstone''s architectural lighting gallery reads "The front of a large home using warm white architectural lights to highlight their entrance". It is a brand-made warm white shot where the light frames the entry. The house is large rather than a bungalow, so copy the warm, even wash and the focus on the entry, not the house. Not viewed: caption confirmed on the page and the link works.', 'Same as the Astoria entry above: warm white only, porch light off, three-quarter angle from the end of the driveway, 2x, expose for the lit wall.'),
  ('phone_control.hero', 1, 'https://images.reviewed.com/fit-in/972x0/filters:strip_icc():strip_exif():no_upscale():format(webp):quality(58)/1692386910735/goveeoutdoors_hero.jpg', 'https://reviewed.com/smarthome/content/govee-permanent-outdoor-lights-review', 'Govee (the brand''s marketing image, credited "Govee" as the hero of Reviewed''s review)', 'The alt text reads "A house lit up by Govee lights and a phone displaying the Govee app." That is both parts of this slot in one frame: the app screen is the subject and the lit house is the background. It is Govee marketing, so it is probably in colour and may be a composite. Copy the layout only, not the colours or the DIY product. Not viewed: I confirmed the alt text and the credit on the page, and the link returns 200.', 'Shoot 15 to 30 minutes after sunset, with the house on steady warm white. Stand on the front walk about 8 m back and use 2x. Hold the phone in the lower right third, screen toward the camera, with the app''s looks screen open and readable, at about half brightness so it doesn''t glare. Tap to focus on the screen, so the house behind goes soft and its roofline crosses the upper third. Turn on Do Not Disturb, remove rings and watch, crop out the app''s name if it shows, and keep the house number out of frame.'),
  ('phone_control.hero', 2, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/EDITED-9314-1024x683.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/why-you-need-cloud-enabled-led-smart-lights-for-your-home/', 'Gemstone Lights (brand blog)', 'Backup, and a real person rather than a possible composite. The caption reads "An over the shoulder view of a women using the Gemstone Lights HUB app". Shot from behind the user, the screen faces the camera, which is the same idea as screen to camera. It comes from a premium brand. Not viewed: I confirmed the caption on the page, but I could not confirm whether her lit house is in the background.', 'An over-the-shoulder version of the main shot. Stand just behind and to the left of the person holding the phone, with their right shoulder soft in the lower right corner. Show the app''s looks screen at half brightness, focus on the screen, and keep the warm white house softly blurred behind with the roofline crossing the upper third. Same rules as above: no notifications, rings, watch or house number.'),
  ('gallery.bungalow', 1, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/bungalow-with-natural-white-lights-1024x800.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/warm-white-or-cool-white-leds/', 'Gemstone Lights (brand blog: warm white or cool white)', 'This is the only brand photo I found captioned as a bungalow ("bungalow with natural white lights"). Gemstone started in Alberta, so its house types are closest to Calgary''s. It is natural white, which is cooler, so copy the framing and how much of the house it shows, not the colour. The same page''s warm white example (https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/IMG_3048-copy-1024x683.jpg, "A beautiful home using Gemstone Lights warm white architectural lighting") is the colour target. Not viewed: captions confirmed on the page and the link returns 200.', 'Shoot at blue hour, about 20 to 35 minutes after sunset while the sky is still deep blue, with steady warm white. Choose a bungalow with a low hip roof. Stand across the street about 20 degrees off straight-on, so the front eave and one side return both show, and use 1x on a tripod. Show the whole front eave and garage line lit, with the house sitting low in the frame and some blue sky above. Expose so the sky stays blue and the soffit wash doesn''t burn out.'),
  ('gallery.bungalow', 2, 'https://astorialightingco.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/unnamed-1-scaled.jpg', 'https://astorialightingco.com/gallery/', 'Astoria Lighting Co. (installer gallery)', 'Backup. The caption reads "A one-story house at night, illuminated by Astoria Lighting. The house has a gray exterior with a wooden garage door and a light-colored front door." It is a real install on a single-storey house with the garage and front door in view, the closest match to a Calgary bungalow in an installer gallery. The colour temperature isn''t stated. Not viewed: caption confirmed on the page and the link returns 200.', 'Same as the Gemstone bungalow entry above: blue hour, warm white, about 20 degrees off straight-on from across the street, 1x, whole front eave and garage lit.'),
  ('gallery.garage', 1, 'https://astorialightingco.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/soffit-sample_1-scaled.jpg', 'https://astorialightingco.com/solutions/soffit-lighting/', 'Astoria Lighting Co. (installer, Product Showcase)', 'The caption reads "Two-story garage with engineered wood exterior featuring Astoria''s closed-track soffit lighting system, displaying warm white light that illuminates the garage entryway and side door". It is a tight garage shot in warm white from a track under the soffit, which is this slot''s subject: the individual points and the wash they throw on the garage face. Not viewed: caption confirmed on the page and the link returns 200.', 'Shoot at blue hour with steady warm white, standing at the end of the driveway. Frame the garage eave and the front door together, close enough that each light point reads on its own and the wash falling down the garage door is the main subject. Use 1x or 2x, whichever fits without cutting the eave, on a tripod. Expose for the brightest part of the garage door so the gradient from the eave down is kept, and turn the porch light off.'),
  ('gallery.garage', 2, 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/06/DSC_6672-1024x681.jpg', 'https://www.gemstonelights.com/outdoor-led-lighting-the-advantages/', 'Gemstone Lights (brand blog)', 'Backup. The caption reads "A close-up of the roofline of a home illuminating warm white lights". It is a brand close-up in warm white that shows how near the camera has to be before the individual points read. Not viewed: caption confirmed on the page and the link returns 200. Another option, if a garage-specific frame is wanted: Trimlight''s "Outline Garages Without Harsh Spots" image (https://trimlight.com/hs-fs/hubfs/outline-garages-without-harsh-spots%20(1).jpg) on https://trimlight.com/blog/13-places-trimlight-helps-you-add-subtle-security-without-harsh-floodlights. Its colour is not stated.', 'Same as the Astoria garage entry above. If the points blur together, step closer or go to 2x until the space between nodes is visible along the eave.'),
  ('install_day.more', 1, 'https://framerusercontent.com/images/pLyOnPj92XAbzwg8FPGLDOJPsI.png', 'https://www.jellyfishlighting.com/manual-internal', 'JellyFish Lighting (internal controller installation manual)', 'None of the brands I checked (JellyFish, Trimlight, Gemstone, Oelo, EverLights, Govee) publishes a photo of a finished controller beside its outlet. This manual comes closest. Its "Location selection" step pictures where the controller goes, with the rule "Choose an interior wall at least 3 inches from any corner and 2 feet 4 inches from the ground". It also says to mount the controller level and plug the power supply into a standard 110V outlet. The image is a PNG and may be a drawing rather than a photo. Trimlight''s support answer (https://trimlight.com/support/where-does-the-trimlight-controller-box-go: "mounted on an interior wall inside a garage... near a standard outlet") and Gemstone''s install guide (https://www.gemstonelights.com/gemstone-lights-track-installation/: "connected to a power outlet... garage, side walls or attic") say the same. Not viewed.', 'Shoot by daylight, with garage lights on and the door open for even light, and no flash. Stand straight-on about 1 m back with the phone at the box''s height, 1x, level. Frame the control box and the outlet it plugs into, with the cord clipped neatly along the wall between them. Move shelves, bikes and tools so nothing else is on that wall in frame. Wipe the box, close its cover and square it to the frame edges.'),
  ('install_day.more', 2, 'https://the-gadgeteer.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/govee-permanent-outdoor-lights-01.jpg', 'https://the-gadgeteer.com/2023/07/31/govee-permanent-outdoor-lights-review-never-climb-that-ladder-again/', 'The Gadgeteer (homeowner review of Govee Permanent Outdoor Lights)', 'Backup. This is a real photo, and the text right under it reads "Pictured above is the control box and AC adaptor, this gave me the reach to get to an outlet in the back of our house." It shows the parts this slot needs in one frame (box, adapter, outlet), but it is a DIY install. Use it as a checklist of what to include, not as the finish to copy: Vesper''s version should look tidier. Not viewed: text confirmed on the page and the link returns 200.', 'Same as the JellyFish entry above. Make sure the box, the plug in the outlet and the cord between them are all visible, and that the cord run looks deliberate (clipped, straight, no loops).');
